cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Moral Compass: A Cross-Family Probe of Frontier Model Values"
message: "If you use this dataset or methodology, please cite as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - given-names: "Di"
    family-names: "Xiao"
    email: "xiaodi.larry@gmail.com"
abstract: >-
  A 140-dilemma corpus, 7 alignment experiments, and an interactive site that
  compare frontier language models on everyday moral dilemmas. Each model answers
  in free text with the options hidden; two LLM judges then map answers to
  A/B/C/D. The headline analysis spans 11 comparable models across two families,
  five GPT (gpt-5.5, gpt-5.4, gpt-5.4-nano, gpt-4o, gpt-4o-mini) on Azure OpenAI
  and six Gemini (3.1 Pro, 3.5 Flash, 3.1 Flash-Lite, 2.5 Pro, 2.5 Flash, 2.5
  Flash-Lite) on Google Vertex AI; four Claude models (including Claude Fable 5)
  are included as a flagged, statistics-excluded probe run through the Claude
  Code agent. Inter-judge
  agreement is 85.4%. Findings include systematic GPT-vs-Gemini disagreement on
  21 of 140 dilemmas, an ~26% answer-flip rate under name and gender swaps, and
  divergent per-model dispositions across sycophancy, value-conflict,
  engagement-hacking, evaluation-awareness, sandbagging, Goodhart, and persona
  probes. The two judges are themselves among the subjects; results that turn on
  same-family judging are flagged and excluded from the aggregate numbers.
keywords:
  - "ai alignment"
  - "moral evaluation"
  - "llm benchmark"
  - "cross-family model comparison"
  - "gpt-5"
  - "gemini"
  - "dilemma probe"
